<Include xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       What the bundle hands whichever MSI runs. Included into EVERY MsiPackage element.

       The packages cannot be factored into one - they have different SourceFiles and mutually exclusive
       InstallConditions, and Burn has no loop over a chain - but their contents can, and had to be. Written
       out per package, adding or renaming a setting is a three-site edit that nothing checks: exactly one of
       the packages ever runs on a given machine, so an omission from the x86 list ships as "that setting
       silently does nothing on 32-bit hosts", and CI runs the bundle only on an x64 runner - which cannot
       exercise the x86 or the arm64 list at all.

       The names live in Bundle.wxs (JasForwardedSettings and JasForwardedSecrets), which generates its
       Variable declarations from the same two lists - so the declarations and the forwarding cannot disagree.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- The architecture policy, in one line: whichever package matches the machine may replace an installed
       product of another architecture. The MSI's own gate stays strict for a bare .msi run by hand. -->
  <MsiProperty Name="FORCE_UPGRADE" Value="1" />

  <!-- Each setting is forwarded verbatim from the like-named bundle variable. All default to empty, so an
       unset one reaches the MSI as an empty property and the MSI's own default applies - the bundle restates
       none of those defaults, since two copies of a default value is how they drift apart. -->
  <?foreach JasSetting in $(JasForwardedSettings) ?>
    <MsiProperty Name="$(JasSetting)" Value="[$(JasSetting)]" />
  <?endforeach?>
  <?foreach JasSecret in $(JasForwardedSecrets) ?>
    <MsiProperty Name="$(JasSecret)" Value="[$(JasSecret)]" />
  <?endforeach?>

</Include>
